  "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le 4 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x} & \\le \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{2}{3}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20614-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20614-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
